{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000054","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 5 candidates are direct matches by trait_reported/trait_efo. Endpoint fidelity is best for models whose phenotyping_reported is explicitly a disease endpoint: PGS000053 and PGS000054 ("Familial late-onset Alzheimer's disease (LOAD)") and PGS000334/PGS004918 ("Late-onset Alzheimer’s disease"). PGS002289 has a clearly non-disease endpoint phenotype string (cognition x age interaction), so despite the large validation sample it is less directly aligned to late-onset AD risk as an endpoint.

Among the endpoint-faithful candidates, only PGS000053/PGS000054 provide PRS-specific effect-size evidence (OR per SD): 1.29 (EUR eval) vs 1.73 (Hispanic/Latin American eval). The domain guidance notes familial late-onset forms remain direct matches and that, within the same study family, materially stronger effect sizes can outweigh other weak tie-breaks. PGS000334 reports an R²=0.191 (C+T, covariates unknown), which is unusually high relative to the provided local heritability ceiling (h2_obs=0.0084; h2_liability=0.0269) and thus likely non-comparable/misinterpreted per the heritability sanity-check rules; this limits its usefulness for ranking. PGS004918 reports only a full-model AUROC=0.731 with unknown covariates and very small validation (n=136), which domain guidance says should not be treated as PRS-only evidence and is a weak basis to outrank better-supported disease-endpoint scores.

Therefore PGS000054 is the best-supported direct-match candidate given the strongest available PRS effect size in a disease-endpoint, multi-cohort familial LOAD context. Remaining limitations: key comparable discrimination metrics (PGS-only AUROC/R²) are missing for the leading candidates, and PGS000054’s evaluation ancestry (AMR) differs from several EUR-evaluated alternatives, so the choice cannot be high-confidence from the visible evidence alone."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000054","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 5 candidates are direct matches at the concept level (trait_efo = late-onset Alzheimer's disease). Endpoint fidelity is strongest for models explicitly targeting late-onset AD diagnosis: PGS000053/PGS000054 (familial late-onset AD) and PGS000334/PGS004918 (late-onset AD). PGS002289 is down-ranked because its phenotyping_reported appears to be a cognition-by-age interaction outcome rather than AD case-control status, despite the AD label.

Among the endpoint-faithful candidates, PGS000053 and PGS000054 form a coherent disease-focused study family (same publication, same 21 GWS variants, age/sex covariates) and provide PRS-relevant effect-size evidence (OR per SD). With limited comparable AUC/R2 across the set (most AUC/R2 fields are null; PGS004918 reports only full-model AUROC; PGS000334 reports R²=0.191 which is unusually high for a simple C+T score with unknown covariates and should be treated cautiously), the strongest clean performance-like signal available is the per-SD ORs from the Neurology familial LOAD study. Between those two, PGS000054 has a materially larger reported OR (1.73 vs 1.29) with similar design and multi-record support, so it is the best-supported pick from visible evidence.

Main limitation: lack of PRS-only AUROC/R² for the leading candidates and unclear covariate/metric comparability for alternatives (notably PGS000334’s high R² and PGS004918’s full-model AUROC), so the recommendation cannot be rated high-confidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005262","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 7 candidates are direct matches by phenotype/label alignment to the target concept ‘nodular goiter’: two UKB portability-framework scores for ‘Nontoxic multinodular goiter’ (PGS001814, PGS002022) and three disease-focused multi-cohort scores explicitly for ‘Benign nodular goiter’ (PGS005261–PGS005263). Two additional models (PGS005273–PGS005274) are for ‘thyroid carcinoma vs benign nodular goiter’ and therefore are not endpoint-faithful for predicting nodular goiter itself (they are contrast-case models), so they are down-ranked for this target.

Within the endpoint-faithful set, the 2026 multi-cohort thyroid/benign thyroid disease study family (PGS005261–PGS005263) is favored over the UKB ‘Portability of 245 polygenic scores’ framework family (PGS001814, PGS002022) because it is disease-focused and shows coherent, repeated support across multiple closely related models with large external evaluation (validation_sample_size n=94,651; EVAL: MAE), whereas the UKB framework models show only modest PRS-only evidence via partial-r (~0.024–0.0277) despite large UKB training.

Among PGS005261–PGS005263, PGS005262 is the best-supported on the visible evidence because it has the highest reported AUROC (0.5933 vs 0.5876 and 0.5854) and the strongest reported per-SD effect sizes (OR 1.408, β 0.342), with similar endpoint wording (‘benign nodular goiter’) and comparable validation context to its sister models. Remaining limitation: the only reported discrimination metric appears to be full_model_auc (with covariates listed as ‘Unknown’), and training sample size is not specified (‘N/A’), so PRS-only comparability and potential covariate inflation cannot be assessed from the provided metadata; therefore confidence is moderate and the overall direct-match recommendation is sub-optimal rather than high-quality."}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004908",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 8 candidates are direct matches to the target concept (renal carcinoma / kidney cancer), with several explicitly aligned by trait_efo (renal carcinoma) or the clinically dominant subtype (renal cell carcinoma). Among them, PGS004908 is best supported by the visible evidence package: (i) strong phenotype alignment (phenotyping_reported: Kidney cancer; trait_efo: renal carcinoma), (ii) the strongest reported discriminative metric in the set (AUROC 0.74, European evaluation) alongside a strong per-SD effect size (OR 1.5, 95% CI 1.43–1.58), (iii) large European validation sample size (n=324,805), and (iv) disease-focused GWAS context (multi-ancestry kidney cancer GWAS identifying susceptibility regions) rather than a sparse portability/pan-trait framework.\n\nHowever, confidence is not High because many candidates only report full-model AUROC (not explicitly PGS-only) and covariate sets differ/are sometimes unknown, limiting cross-model comparability; additionally, other models (e.g., PGS000076/PGS000787) show similar full-model AUROC (~0.722) with very large validation (n=391,610), leaving some residual ambiguity about PRS-only contribution and the fairest ranking basis from the provided metadata."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS003381",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "Among the 14 retrieved candidates, the strongest direct-match evidence comes from endometrial-cancer models, which domain_knowledge explicitly treats as the clinically dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma (~90% of cases) and thus a strong semantic match. PGS003381 has high endpoint fidelity (phenotyping_reported: \"uterine endometrial carcinoma\"), European evaluation ancestry, large validation sample (n=144,479), and a reasonably strong reported discrimination package (full_model_auc=0.761; R²=0.11) with only basic covariates (age, top 20 PCs), making its performance evidence more comparable than models relying on heavier clinical covariates. The exact-label uterine carcinoma model (PGS001795) is endpoint-aligned but comes from a global biobank framework paper and shows weaker reported metrics (AUROC=0.66; PRS-only Nagelkerke R²=0.001948), so it is not best-supported versus the dominant-subtype, disease-anchored alternative. Main limitation: for PGS003381 the visible AUC/R² appear to be full-model (not explicitly PRS-only), so PRS-only contribution is uncertain, lowering confidence from High."
}
